    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Mickey Moniak** (74.73) and **Corbin Carroll** (72.25) stand out as our most potent options. **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive park-factor boost and a prime #2 spot in the order against Sonny Gray, making him an elite play despite the pitcher\u2019s competence. Meanwhile, **Corbin Carroll** remains a foundational asset; despite a minor park penalty, his matchup against Leahy creates the perfect environment to leverage his elite sprint speed for stolen base production. **Josh Naylor** (69.39) rounds out our top tier, as his strong platoon advantage against Mitch Keller keeps his floor high in a favorable offensive setup.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved leaving **Zach McKinstry** (51.70) on the bench. While his dynamic platoon edge and favorable park factor made him a compelling option, the sheer density of higher-efficiency hitters in our lineup pushed him out. Similarly, while **Jose Caballero** remains a coveted stolen base threat, he was benched due to a significant negative platoon adjustment against Mize, which severely hampered his expected output compared to the rest of the utility corps. \n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the risk profile of **James Wood** (54.86). Although his xwOBA profile is elite, he faces Jesus Luzardo, whose ability to hold runners and quick delivery creates a challenging environment for our stolen base strategy. We are also monitoring the lower-order volatility of **Max Muncy** (53.88) and **Henry Bolte** (54.47), both of whom are facing Robbie Ray; while the platoon advantage is present, their low batting order positions keep them firmly in the \"marginal\" scoring tier, requiring high-leverage outcomes to justify their inclusion over potential reinforcements like **Travis Bazzana**.",
